;;; DRAFT -- set-cdr!
;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; AUTHORED, not model-seeded. The drafting batch
;;; (scratch/pairs/draft_records.py, see draft_run.log) skipped
;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/set-cdr-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "set-cdr!"
  :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(set-cdr! pair obj) -> unspecified"
  :summary "Replace the cdr of a pair in place, SHARING the new tail rather than copying it. This is the verb that makes lists a mutable structure."
  :explanation "set-cdr! writes one field, exactly as set-car! does, but the field it writes is the one that holds the structure together -- so this single verb is what turns a list from a value into a mutable data structure. Everything in this chapter's linear-update family is set-cdr! underneath. The requirement that matters most is one people skip: the new tail is SHARED, not copied. After (set-cdr! p t), (eq? (cdr p) t) must hold. The write lands in place, on the caller's own cell: the pair keeps its identity, every other holder of it sees the new tail without being told, and the tail that was there before is no longer reachable through p. That is what makes constant-time extension possible, what makes append! able to embed its arguments, and what makes a circular list expressible at all -- write a cell into its own cdr and the list has no end. There is no direct test in the conformance suite, but list.sld:402-410 exercises it hard inside the pair-fold test: kons calls (set-cdr! pair tail) and the reversal that results is checked to be built from the original cells, which is only true if the write landed on the caller's cell with the tail shared. Motoi cannot do this today on a proper list -- there is no pair to write into, and the existing record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat documents the tail being COPIED, which is precisely the divergence that makes queues, circular lists and every linear-update verb impossible."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define p (cons 1 2))\n(set-cdr! p 99)\n(display p)\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; the new tail is SHARED, not copied.\n(define p (cons 1 '()))\n(define t (list 2 3))\n(set-cdr! p t)\n(display (list 'p p 'tail-shared (eq? (cdr p) t)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; a cell whose cdr is itself: a list with no end. Do not walk it.\n(define ring (list 1))\n(set-cdr! ring ring)\n(display (list 'first (car ring) 'second (cadr ring) 'wraps (eq? (cdr ring) ring)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The new tail is SHARED, not copied. (eq? (cdr p) obj) must hold after the call. Everything in the linear-update family depends on this."
             "Any object may become the cdr, not only a list. That is how dotted pairs and improper lists are made."
             "Writing a cell into its own cdr, directly or through a chain, makes a circular list. Nothing warns you and length will not return."
             "The return value is unspecified."
             "Literal pairs from quoted data must not be mutated."
             "MOTOI DIVERGES TODAY: on a list built by list, the new tail is COPIED rather than shared (documented in scheme/reference/39-list.slat, which carries :status \"needs-alfred\"). That single divergence is why queues, circular lists, last-pair handles and all 15 linear-update verbs cannot work.")
  :drawbacks ("It can build a structure that no other verb in the language can safely consume, and it does so silently."
             "It is the sharpest tool here: one wrong write reroutes an arbitrary amount of shared structure."
             "A cycle created by accident is usually discovered as a hang, at the worst possible moment.")
  :usecases ("Constant-time list extension through a kept last-pair handle."
             "Queues, ring buffers and any structure with a tail pointer."
             "Implementing the linear-update verbs; every one of them is a set-cdr! in a loop.")
  :related ("set-car!"
             "cons"
             "cdr"
             "last-pair"
             "circular-list")
  :learn (:concept "The write that makes a list a structure"
          :prerequisites ("set-car!" "cons" "last-pair" "eq?")
          :progression "Extend a list through last-pair, then build a two-element ring by hand, then predict what length would do to it. Stop before running that last one.")
  :provenance "R7RS-small 6.4 (cited spec/R7RS-SMALL-PRIMITIVE-FLOOR.slat:193). No dedicated test in scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld (named at :163); the strongest in-repo exercise is inside the pair-fold test at list.sld:402-410, which requires the write to land on the caller's cell with the tail SHARED. An existing record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat carries :status \"needs-alfred\". Drafted 2026-08-03 against the incoming cons-cell representation; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define p (cons 1 '()))\n(set-cdr! p (list 2 3))\n(display p)\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define p (cons 1 '()))\n(define t (list 2 3))\n(set-cdr! p t)\n(display (list 'shared (eq? (cdr p) t) 'car-unchanged (car p)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(set-cdr! (last-pair xs) (list 4))\n(display (list 'extended-in-place xs))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator wants to add to the end of a list without copying it. Candidates: append (copies the whole list), append! (walks to the end every time), or keeping the last cell and writing through it with set-cdr! (one write, constant time). Only the third is actually constant time, and it is constant time only because the tail is SHARED -- if the implementation copied the tail instead, the operation would be linear and the shared structure everything else depends on would not exist."
     :explanation "eng: the list now runs on into the new tail. math: the cdr is the new object by identity and the car is untouched. cs: extending through last-pair is the idiom, and it is the program that currently fails on Motoi."
     :meta "The sharing requirement looks like an implementation detail and is actually the specification. Copying the tail satisfies every equal? test and breaks the language.")
    (:eng "The requirement that looks like a detail is the one that carries the structure. Sharing is not an optimisation here."
     :math "Identity of the new cdr with the object passed in is the assertion; equality is not enough."
     :cs "This one write is the difference between a list library and a list data structure.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Changing what a link points at, and so changing the shape of everything downstream."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(define p (cons 1 '()))\n(set-cdr! p (list 2))\n(display p)\n(newline)")
             (:domain "re-setting the points at a junction" :code "(define junction (cons 'signal '()))\n(set-cdr! junction (list 'branch-line))\n(display junction)\n(newline)")
             (:domain "redirecting a forwarding address" :code "(define entry (cons 'name '()))\n(set-cdr! entry (list 'new-address))\n(display entry)\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, the cdr is rewritten and everything after that point changes for every traveller. Re-setting the points at a junction sends every following train down a different line without touching either line. Redirecting a forwarding address changes where everything downstream goes while the entry itself stays where it was. All three share a small local change with an unbounded downstream effect."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever one connection determines an entire path.")
    (:eng "A one-word change to a route changes every journey that uses it."
     :math "The reachable set from a cell is determined by its cdr, so rewriting the cdr rewrites the set."
     :cs "Pointer rewriting is how every linked structure is edited, and why every linked structure needs an ownership story.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define p (cons 1 '()))\n(set-cdr! p (list 2 3))\n(display (list 'p p 'len (length p)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define p (cons 1 '()))\n(define t (list 2 3))\n(set-cdr! p t)\n(display (list 'shared-not-copied (eq? (cdr p) t)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define ring (list 1))\n(set-cdr! ring ring)\n(display (list 'self-referential (eq? (cdr ring) ring) 'second-element (cadr ring)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "set-cdr! replaces the cdr field of the given pair with obj, sharing obj rather than copying it: (eq? (cdr p) obj) holds afterwards. The pair's identity and its car are unchanged. obj may be any object, including the pair itself, which produces a circular list."
     :invariant "forall pair p, object o. after set-cdr!(p, o): eq?(cdr(p), o) and car(p) is unchanged and p is unchanged by identity"
     :meta "The pattern is structural assignment. What would falsify the claim: a copied tail -- which is exactly what Motoi does today on a proper list -- a changed car, or an inability to write the pair into itself. The self-referential program is the sharpest of the three, because a copying implementation cannot even express it: copying a list into its own cdr terminates and produces something finite and wrong.")
    (:eng "A copying implementation cannot build a ring. That is the one-line test for whether the representation is real."
     :math "eq? is the assertion. Any test written with equal? passes on the broken implementation."
     :cs "The self-referential case is not an edge case; it is the case that proves the field is a pointer.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (extend! xs y) (set-cdr! (last-pair xs) (list y)) xs)\n(display (extend! (list 1 2) 3))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (make-ring n)\n  (let ((r (list n)))\n    (set-cdr! r r)\n    r))\n(define r (make-ring 7))\n(display (list (car r) (cadr r) (caddr r)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (make-queue)\n  (let ((head (list 'sentinel)))\n    (cons head head)))\n(define (enqueue! q v)\n  (let ((cell (list v)))\n    (set-cdr! (cdr q) cell)\n    (set-cdr! q cell)\n    q))\n(define q (make-queue))\n(enqueue! q 1)\n(enqueue! q 2)\n(display (cdr (car q)))\n(newline)"
     :base "set-cdr! writes a shared tail into the second field of an existing pair."
     :emergent "eng: constant-time extension. math: a cell pointing at itself is the smallest possible circular list, and it is one line. cs: a queue with a sentinel head and a tail pointer -- two set-cdr! calls per enqueue, constant time, and the structure every scheduler and buffer in the system is built from. None of these three programs can work if the tail is copied."
     :meta "The family is mutable linked structure. What emerges is the full cost of the current representation: not one broken verb but a whole class of data structures that cannot be written at all, which is the case for changing it.")
    (:eng "This is the argument for the change, in three programs: extension, rings and queues. All three need a shared tail."
     :math "Self-reference in one line is what a pointer field buys; nothing weaker gives it to you."
     :cs "Queues, rings and builders are the everyday structures, and every one of them is set-cdr! with a tail pointer.")
  )
)
